Subject: [PATCH 14/14] web: the bridge a browser host drives PPSSPP through

The whole of the C++ this port adds, and it is small because everything it
needs already exists. The seam it implements is specified in JavaScript rather
than here: ../../src/module.ts declares the four entry points and the shape of
every event, and the SDK is written against that declaration alone.

    int  ppsspp_web_apply_setting(const char *key, const char *value);
    void ppsspp_web_pause(void);
    void ppsspp_web_resume(void);
    void ppsspp_web_shutdown(void);

**Settings.** `Section/Key` in PPSSPP's own ini vocabulary, and the value as a
string. Core/Config.cpp already holds the table this needs — g_sectionMeta maps
a section name to its ConfigSetting array — and ConfigSetting::ReadFromIniSection
already knows how to turn a string into whichever type a setting is. So
SetConfigValueFromString is a lookup plus a one-entry ini section to read the
value out of, rather than a second copy of the type switch. Keys under `Web/`
are this package's own and have no ini entry: `Web/FastForward` sets
PSP_CoreParameter().fastForward, which is what the throttle actually reads, and
is the same thing the WebSocket debugger's GameSubscriber does.

**Pause.** Core_Break(BreakReason::UIPause) and Core_Resume, which is how PPSSPP
itself implements a pause with no menu — see VIRTKEY_PAUSE_NO_MENU in
UI/EmuScreen.cpp. Emulation stops, the last frame stays on the canvas, and no
screen is pushed, so the host's own pause UI is the only one a player sees.

**Events go one way and from one place.** PpssppWeb::Frame() is called from
SDLMain's Emscripten main loop, which means the browser's main thread — the only
thread where a call into JS is legal and where the page's callback can run. What
can be observed is polled there (a boot completing, the frame rate, PPSSPP's own
pause menu, the frontend returning to its game list); what happens on another
thread is queued and drained there. That is why there is no
MAIN_THREAD_ASYNC_EM_ASM anywhere in this patch: nothing emits from a thread
that would need it.

Two producers are not observable and are hooked instead, both under
__EMSCRIPTEN__ and both three lines: a failed boot in Core/System.cpp, at the
one point where the error string still exists before the caller consumes it and
the boot state goes back to Off; and Core/SaveState.cpp, where SaveSlot and
LoadSlot wrap the caller's callback so a host hears about save states however
they were triggered, including from PPSSPP's own menu.

`booted` is the one with a hard requirement, because it is what resolves the
promise start() returned: it means a game is up, not that main() ran. The test
is the boot having completed *and* the frontend having switched to it, which is
the point at which the next frame drawn is the game's.

Every event is built as an object in JS from arguments that cross as numbers and
pointers, so nothing here has to escape a game title into JSON.

`pause` and `resume` are emitted for PPSSPP's *own* pause menu and nothing else.
The ones this package triggers through ppsspp_web_pause are emitted on the JS
side, and emitting them here as well would have a host see every one twice.

**ENV**, alongside the four functions in -sEXPORTED_RUNTIME_METHODS, is for the
other half of the seam: SDL3's Emscripten video driver resolves
SDL_HINT_EMSCRIPTEN_CANVAS_SELECTOR through SDL_GetHint, which reads the
environment first, and this package builds a new canvas per session so they
cannot all be called `#canvas`. No C is needed for it — the SDK writes the
selector into ENV before callMain.

